// DecideRefusal Why a user may not decide a duplicate group, change its decision, vote on, withdraw or dismiss its
// pending decision, or propose to stop its series allow.
type DecideRefusal string

// DecisionState top-changes rail decision-state filter (frontend spec §5.1 / backend
// ticket §7.1). Slices the open-review change pool by the reviewer's
// change-level verdict (“DataActionReview.decision“).
//
// Since the cell-veto ruling (2026-07-24, D3) “reject“ and
// “request_changes“ are ONE thing at cell level -- both block release, both
// fold into “rejected_count“. The members that follow from that:
//
//   - “undecided“ = NO ledger row for the history_id. A “comment_only“ row
//     is therefore NOT undecided here (a DELIBERATE divergence from
//     “decision_counts“, which folds it into undecided). Do NOT widen it.
//   - “rejected“ = ANY blocking verdict (“reject“ OR “request_changes“),
//     so the chip and “rejected_count“ select the same set (D5). It
//     deliberately OVERLAPS “changes_requested“, which stays the finer
//     “request_changes“-only slice. The rail's states were never a partition.
//   - “unresolved“ / “decided“ = the GATE-ALIGNED pair, matching
//     “decision_counts“ and “resolve_releasable_rows“: “approve“ and both
//     blocking verdicts decide, so “unresolved“ = no ledger row OR
//     “comment_only“, and “decided“ = the three verdicts. They are the ONLY
//     members that are DISJOINT and EXHAUSTIVE over “all“. “unresolved“ is
//     the only state other than “all“ from which a “comment_only“ change is
//     reachable.
//
// “all“ = no decision predicate. “accepted“ is the PRODUCT label for
// “ReviewDecision.approve“; the API keeps “accepted“ and maps internally
// -- “approve“ is never exposed as a decision_state value.
type DecisionState string

